<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Ramnad Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1605 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Ramanathapuram</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of the Sethupathi chiefs of Ramanathapuram, guardians of the pilgrimage route to Rameswaram.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maravar">Maravar</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadaikka-thevar">Sadaikka Thevar</a> was recognised as Sethupathi, protector of the causeway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rameswaram">Rameswaram</a>, by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/madurai-nayak-dynasty">Madurai Nayak dynasty</a> around 1605. His successors ruled the dry coastal region of Ramnad as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizhavan-sethupathi">Kizhavan Sethupathi</a> asserted independence in the late 17th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Sethupathis led a warrior society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maravar">Maravar</a> clans and controlled the pilgrim route and pearl fisheries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palk-strait">Palk Strait</a>. They endowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanathaswamy-temple">Ramanathaswamy</a> temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rameswaram">Rameswaram</a>, building its long corridors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizhavan-sethupathi">Kizhavan Sethupathi</a> persecuted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> converts and executed the missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-de-britto">John de Britto</a> in 1693.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ramnad lost territory to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> and the Sivaganga chiefdom in the 18th century. After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/carnatic-sultanate">Nawabs of Arcot</a> and then the British took control, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">British East India Company</a> deposed the Sethupathi in the 1790s and turned Ramnad into a zamindari estate in 1803.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ramnad-kingdom.jpg" alt="Ramnad Kingdom" width="320" height="482"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Venu62 at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thanjavur_temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1605 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ramanathapuram</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sadaikka Thevar, Kizhavan Sethupathi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tamil</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sethupathis of Ramnad, Maravar kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1704&amp;focus=ramnad-kingdom" aria-label="Ramnad Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6856 1827 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ramnad Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7176 2083L7154 2063M7176 2083L7364 1601"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7154 2063h0M7364 1601h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7176 2083h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7176 2083h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ramanathapuram. <a href="/atlas?year=1704&amp;focus=ramnad-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

